\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.12}{\ignorespaces Scheme of the PDH sidebands addressing the atomic resonance, shown at two snapshots of the laser frequency scan. The atomic transition sits at the fixed frequency $f_0$ (green dashed line). In black: the carrier is scanned onto resonance ($f_0'=f_0$), producing the true photoassociation signal. In purple: the carrier is instead detuned to $f_0'=f_0+f_M$, so that it is itself off-resonance, but its lower sideband coincides with $f_0$ and drives a spurious ("ghost") loss resonance. As the scan sweeps $f_0'$, this coincidence recurs whenever $f_0' = f_0 \pm f_M$, producing satellite peaks such as the one reported in Fig.~\ref {fig:raie-40mhz}.}}{84}{figure.caption.240}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.13}{\ignorespaces Atomic loss due to the FP cavity's PDH sideband addressing the $^1S_0 \rightarrow {}^3P_1$ line at $\Delta = f_0 - f_M$, with $f_M$ the modulation frequency of the PDH EOM. The dashed line marks the expected detuning of the ghost resonance, $|\Delta |=f_M=40.48$~MHz.}}{85}{figure.caption.241}%
